Asterix the samurai just made it all the way up to the penthouse and
beyond, and would like to make a few points:

1) Would some devteamer or other divinity please explain to me what the
purpose of the drag-the-bugger-down-a-level-or-two-if-he-has-the-AoY
feature is? Seriously: I've discovered that most annoying features of
nethack are there to prevent some form of abuse or another, but this one
stumps me. If the idea is that it makes the game harder, I think not,
it only taxes your patience. (Far and away the most important stat in
Gehennom is getting your Pat up to 18/**. Esp for Lawfuls.)

2) I thought that when you were made HoE you always got Excalibur
if it wasn't already in the game? Asterix didn't. Not that he minded:
the b+6ToM is far and away the most fearsome weapon I've ever seen.
Sliced the WoY in two on several occasions. "Double trouble eh? I'll
show _you_ double trouble!"

3) The exp levels are spaced so far apart as to be meaningless. This is
Asterix's status line at ascension:

Asterix the Daimyo     St:25 Dx:17 Co:18 In:17 Wi:20 Ch:10  Lawful
Astral Plane $:912 HP:152(199) Pw:116(158) AC:-24 Xp:23/30111791 Stressed

Ok: so he ate something that brought him to lvl 23 and a round 3M
points. This happened in the sanctum. 59 dungeon levels and
111791 pts worth of kills later, he's still lvl 23! So it's pretty much
impossible to gain levels the old-fashioned away after a point and you
have to do it by other means. You're pretty much invulnerable by then
excpet to instant death, so the HPs don't matter. Your final score only
reflects your _real_ experience (i.e earned the old-fashioned way.) So
why gain levels at all?

I suggest spacing the levels closer (_much_ closer) together. Eliminate
the underhanded methods of level gain. Make it so you can get certain
intrinsics _only_ by reaching the appropriate level. Or reset the prayer
counter, and new toys from your deity every time you gain a level.

4) I've now ascended 4 classes (BVPS) in 3 alignments, and I have to say
much of the game's charm evaporates beyond the quest. By the time you've
done the castle, no matter what you started out as, you have pretty much
same stats, same intrinsics, same stuff (ok so the samurai slicer-dicer
and the valkyrie yoyo are a bit different) and it's difficult to tell
them apart. I wonder if I'd ever play, say, the Healer class beyond the
quest. The only thing different about the Healer is the toys she gets
at t=0, and they're not difficult to come by.

I suggest giving every class different functions to make you play them
differently in later stages. I suggested this before with some new
classes: a Yogi class who can regenerate by meditating, and gets a third
eye when becoming HoE to gaze piercingly (a la Baalzebub) at passersby.
And the Flowerchild whose god is never happier than when his acolyte is
hallucinating. Something like that could be implemented fairly easily
for existing classes as well. Perhaps kill points could be computed
differently depending on your class. A Wizard gets extra credit for
using spells, and doesn't become hungry as fast. An archaeologist for
using wands. Rogues get the nymph's intrinsic at level 21. And so on.

Just throwing round some ideas. Don't mistake me though, it's a great
game and even though I'm complaining, I'm not ready to retire yet.

